  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAfter a breezy and comfortable summer day today, humid \nconditions return tomorrow morning across eastern New York and \nwestern New England as a warm front moves through. Additionally \nat the upper levels, a shortwave trough moves through helping \ncontribute to a favorable environment for thunderstorms to \ndevelop tomorrow afternoon and evening across eastern New York \nand western New England. Latest high resolution forecast models \nare suggesting timing for thunderstorms and rain showers is \nbetween 1 PM and midnight tomorrow. \n\nThe latest forecast supports thunderstorms to develop for \nlocations south of the Mohawk Valley to the Greater Capital \nRegion, including the eastern Catskills, Mid-Hudson Valley, \nTaconics, and Litchfield Hills, during the afternoon hours. With\nplenty of daytime heating, moisture from the associated warm \nfront, and shortwave trough to move through, strong to severe \nthunderstorms could develop for those locations during the \nafternoon hours tomorrow. Taking a deeper dive into the \nenvironment, energy and instability needed for strong to severe \nstorms is present on the latest high resolution forecast models.\nA Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for isolated severe \nthunderstorms tomorrow has been introduced for locations south\nof the I-90 corridor from the Storm Prediction Center. With \nlatest forecast trends on the environment for tomorrow for those\nlocations, this increases forecast confidence for isolated \nsevere thunderstorms to occur tomorrow afternoon. \n\nThe primary hazard is going to be strong to damaging wind gusts\nwith severe thunderstorms. One thing to note, the ground \ncontinues to be saturated across these locations, so strong wind\ngusts over 50 mph may be all it takes to uproot trees and \ndamage trees. Heavy downpours can also occur with thunderstorms \ntomorrow as enough moisture and energy above the surface is \nhelping contribute to the potential of isolated instances of \nflash flooding from thunderstorms tomorrow afternoon and early \nevening. As the sun sets tomorrow evening, energy and \ninstability decreases helping contribute to weaken the \nenvironment needed for strong storms south of the Greater \nCapital Region. For locations northward, we will need to \ncontinue to monitor trends as an isolated strong storm can \ndevelop during the overnight hours as a cold front starts to \npush eastward into eastern New York. \n\nA low pressure system over the Great Lakes region is currently \nsupported to move north and east into Canada, but brings a cold \nfront to move through eastern New York and western New England \nduring the overnight hours Monday into early Tuesday morning. \nThe cold front brings relief from the humidity and dry \nconditions are in store behind it for Tuesday.",
